## Chat Condenser -- Implementation Plan

### 1. Understanding the Data Flow

Here is how messages currently flow through the system:

1. Claude CLI sends NDJSON `assistant` messages to the WS server (`/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/server/ws.ts`)
2. Server relays them as `ServerRelay` to the browser
3. `useWebSocket` hook (`/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/hooks/use-websocket.ts`) receives them in `ws.onmessage`
4. `relayChatMessage()` calls `extractText()` to convert `ClaudeAssistant` into `ChatMessage`
5. `extractText()` maps each content block: text blocks become their text, `tool_use` blocks become `[tool_use: Grep]`, and `tool_result` blocks become `[tool_result: ...]`
6. These all get joined with newlines into a single `content` string in one `ChatMessage`
7. Chat.tsx renders each `ChatMessage` as a `<Message>` bubble

**Key insight**: A single `ClaudeAssistant` message can contain a mix of text blocks AND tool blocks. The `extractText()` function flattens them all into one string. So a "tool-only" message is one where `content` consists entirely of `[tool_use: ...]` and `[tool_result: ...]` lines with no actual Claude text.

### 2. Detecting "Tool-Only" Messages

The detection must happen at the `ChatMessage` level (since we operate on the rendering layer, not the raw data). A message is "tool-only" when:

- `role === "assistant"` (not system, not user)
- No `controlRequest` or `userQuestion` property
- `content` consists entirely of lines matching `[tool_use: <name>]` and/or `[tool_result: <content>]`

The best approach is a regex-based check on the content string, since by the time we're in the rendering layer, the original `AssistantContentBlock[]` array has been flattened.

However, there is a **better alternative**: enrich `ChatMessage` at creation time with metadata about what tool calls it contains, so the rendering layer does not need to reverse-parse the content string. This stays within the "rendering layer" constraint if we add the metadata as a non-breaking optional field.

**Recommended approach**: Add an optional `toolCalls` field to `ChatMessage` that `relayChatMessage()` populates when producing assistant messages. This keeps detection clean and avoids fragile regex parsing. The raw data is unaffected since `rawMessages` is a separate array.

### 3. Type Changes

Add to `ChatMessage` in `/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/lib/types.ts`:

```ts
export interface ToolCallInfo {
  /** Tool name, e.g. "Grep", "Read", "Bash" */
  name: string;
  /** The tool_use block id, if available */
  toolUseId?: string;
}

export interface ChatMessage {
  id: string;
  role: ChatMessageRole;
  content: string;
  timestamp: number;
  controlRequest?: { /* ... existing ... */ };
  userQuestion?: { /* ... existing ... */ };
  /** Present on assistant messages. Lists tool calls found in this message. Empty if none. */
  toolCalls?: ToolCallInfo[];
  /** True if this message has ONLY tool_use/tool_result blocks and no text content. */
  isToolOnly?: boolean;
}
```

### 4. Enriching Messages in `relayChatMessage()`

In `/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/hooks/use-websocket.ts`, modify the assistant branch of `relayChatMessage()` to populate `toolCalls` and `isToolOnly`:

```ts
if (msg.type === "assistant") {
  const assistant = msg as ClaudeAssistant;
  const blocks = assistant.message?.content ?? [];

  const textBlocks = blocks.filter((b) => b.type === "text" && b.text.trim());
  const toolUseBlocks = blocks.filter((b) => b.type === "tool_use");

  const toolCalls: ToolCallInfo[] = toolUseBlocks.map((b) => ({
    name: (b as { name: string }).name,
    toolUseId: (b as { id: string }).id,
  }));

  const text = extractText(assistant);
  if (!text) return null;

  const isToolOnly = textBlocks.length === 0 && toolCalls.length > 0;

  return {
    id: nextId(),
    role: "assistant",
    content: text,
    timestamp: Date.now(),
    toolCalls,
    isToolOnly,
  };
}
```

This is a minimal, non-breaking change. Existing code that does not check `toolCalls` or `isToolOnly` is entirely unaffected.

### 5. Grouping Logic -- Pure Utility Function

Create a new file `/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/lib/condense-messages.ts`. This is a pure function that takes `ChatMessage[]` and returns a new array where consecutive tool-only messages are replaced by group markers:

```ts
/** A regular message, passed through unchanged. */
export interface SingleItem {
  kind: "single";
  message: ChatMessage;
}

/** A group of consecutive tool-only messages, condensed into one row. */
export interface CondensedGroup {
  kind: "condensed";
  /** Unique key for React rendering, derived from first message ID */
  id: string;
  /** All the original messages in this group */
  messages: ChatMessage[];
  /** Summary like "5 tool calls" */
  summary: string;
  /** Breakdown like { Grep: 3, Read: 2 } */
  toolCounts: Record<string, number>;
  /** Total number of messages condensed */
  count: number;
  /** Timestamp of the first message in the group */
  timestamp: number;
}

export type DisplayItem = SingleItem | CondensedGroup;

export function condenseMessages(messages: ChatMessage[]): DisplayItem[] {
  const result: DisplayItem[] = [];
  let i = 0;

  while (i < messages.length) {
    const msg = messages[i];

    // Check if this starts a tool-only run
    if (msg.isToolOnly) {
      // Collect consecutive tool-only messages
      const group: ChatMessage[] = [];
      while (i < messages.length && messages[i].isToolOnly) {
        group.push(messages[i]);
        i++;
      }

      // Only condense if there are 2+ consecutive tool-only messages.
      // A single tool-only message is shown normally (not worth condensing).
      if (group.length >= 2) {
        const toolCounts: Record<string, number> = {};
        for (const m of group) {
          for (const tc of m.toolCalls ?? []) {
            toolCounts[tc.name] = (toolCounts[tc.name] ?? 0) + 1;
          }
        }

        const breakdown = Object.entries(toolCounts)
          .map(([name, count]) => `${name} x${count}`)
          .join(", ");

        const totalCalls = Object.values(toolCounts).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);

        result.push({
          kind: "condensed",
          id: `group-${group[0].id}`,
          messages: group,
          summary: `${totalCalls} tool calls (${breakdown})`,
          toolCounts,
          count: group.length,
          timestamp: group[0].timestamp,
        });
      } else {
        // Single tool-only message: show as-is
        result.push({ kind: "single", message: group[0] });
      }
    } else {
      result.push({ kind: "single", message: msg });
      i++;
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

Key design decisions:
- **Threshold of 2**: Only condense when there are 2+ consecutive tool-only messages. A single tool call standing alone is not noise.
- **Messages with text content break the sequence**: If Claude writes text between tool calls, those are `SingleItem` and break the consecutive run.
- **`controlRequest` and `userQuestion` messages break the sequence**: They are never `isToolOnly`, so they naturally break the grouping.
- **User messages break the sequence**: They have `role: "user"`, never `isToolOnly`.
- **System messages break the sequence**: Same reasoning.

### 6. The CondensedToolGroup Component

Create `/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/components/dashboard/condensed-tool-group.tsx`:

```tsx
"use client";

import { useState } from "react";
import { ChevronRight } from "lucide-react";
import type { CondensedGroup } from "@/lib/condense-messages";
import { Message } from "./message";
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";

export function CondensedToolGroup({ group }: { group: CondensedGroup }) {
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  return (
    <div className="py-1">
      {/* Collapsed summary bar */}
      <button
        onClick={() => setExpanded((v) => !v)}
        className="flex w-full items-center gap-2 rounded-md px-3 py-1.5 text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:bg-muted/50 transition-colors"
      >
        <ChevronRight
          className={cn(
            "size-3 shrink-0 transition-transform",
            expanded && "rotate-90"
          )}
        />
        <span className="font-mono">{group.summary}</span>
      </button>

      {/* Expanded: show all original messages */}
      {expanded && (
        <div className="ml-5 mt-1 space-y-1 border-l border-muted pl-3">
          {group.messages.map((msg) => (
            <Message key={msg.id} message={msg} />
          ))}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

This follows the existing expand/collapse pattern used by `RawMessage` in `chat.tsx` (the `expanded` state toggle with `useState`). It reuses the existing `Message` component to render each individual message when expanded. The `lucide-react` `ChevronRight` icon (already a dependency) provides the collapse/expand indicator. The border-left + indent provides a visual grouping cue.

### 7. Integrating into Chat.tsx

In `/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/components/dashboard/chat.tsx`, the rendering loop changes from iterating over `messages` directly to iterating over `condenseMessages(messages)`:

```tsx
import { useMemo } from "react";
import { condenseMessages } from "@/lib/condense-messages";
import { CondensedToolGroup } from "./condensed-tool-group";

// Inside the Chat component:
const displayItems = useMemo(() => condenseMessages(messages), [messages]);

// In the JSX, replace the messages.map(...) block:
{displayItems.map((item) => {
  if (item.kind === "condensed") {
    return <CondensedToolGroup key={item.id} group={item} />;
  }

  const msg = item.message;
  return msg.userQuestion ? (
    <UserQuestion key={msg.id} message={msg} onRespond={onRespondToUserQuestion} />
  ) : msg.controlRequest ? (
    <ControlRequest key={msg.id} message={msg} onRespond={onRespondToControl} />
  ) : (
    <Message key={msg.id} message={msg} />
  );
})}
```

The `useMemo` ensures the condensing logic only re-runs when the `messages` array reference changes (which happens on every new message). This is efficient because `condenseMessages` is a single linear pass -- O(n).

### 8. Raw View Unaffected

The raw view (`showRaw` toggle) renders from `rawMessages` (a separate `ClaudeMessage[]`), not from `messages`. The condenser only affects the `messages` rendering path. No changes needed for raw view.

### 9. File Summary and Sequencing

The implementation involves changes to 4 existing files and creation of 2 new files, in this order:

**Step 1 -- Types** (`/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/lib/types.ts`):
- Add `ToolCallInfo` interface
- Add optional `toolCalls?: ToolCallInfo[]` and `isToolOnly?: boolean` fields to `ChatMessage`

**Step 2 -- Message enrichment** (`/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/hooks/use-websocket.ts`):
- Import `ToolCallInfo` type
- Modify the `assistant` branch in `relayChatMessage()` to compute and attach `toolCalls` and `isToolOnly`
- No changes to `extractText()` (it still produces the same display text)

**Step 3 -- Grouping utility** (new file: `/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/lib/condense-messages.ts`):
- Export `SingleItem`, `CondensedGroup`, `DisplayItem` types
- Export `condenseMessages()` pure function

**Step 4 -- Condensed group component** (new file: `/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/components/dashboard/condensed-tool-group.tsx`):
- Collapsible summary bar with expand/collapse
- Reuses existing `Message` component for expanded view

**Step 5 -- Chat integration** (`/Users/chiu.fyi/work/personal/thos/components/dashboard/chat.tsx`):
- Import `condenseMessages` and `CondensedToolGroup`
- Add `useMemo` call to produce `displayItems`
- Replace `messages.map(...)` with `displayItems.map(...)`

### 10. Edge Cases and Considerations

**Mixed messages**: A single `ClaudeAssistant` message might contain both text blocks and tool_use blocks (e.g., Claude says "Let me search for that" and then runs Grep in the same message). These have `isToolOnly === false` because they contain meaningful text. They render as normal messages and break the condensing sequence. This is correct behavior.

**Streaming / incremental updates**: The current architecture already handles this correctly. Each NDJSON line from the CLI produces a new `ChatMessage` (the assistant branch in `relayChatMessage` is called per relay). The last message for a given assistant turn is the complete one (stop_reason is set). The condenser works on the full `messages` array, so as new messages arrive, the condensed groups update naturally.

**Messages with only `tool_result` blocks**: These would have `toolCalls` as empty (since `tool_result` is not `tool_use`), but they would still have `isToolOnly === false` since the check requires `toolCalls.length > 0`. Actually, looking at the code more carefully, `tool_result` blocks also result in content like `[tool_result: ...]`, so `textBlocks.length === 0` would be true and `toolCalls.length > 0` would be false. The message would NOT be marked `isToolOnly`. This means standalone tool_result messages just render normally as before. This is acceptable -- tool_results usually come in the same assistant message as tool_use blocks in practice.

**Performance**: `condenseMessages` is O(n) where n is the number of messages. Wrapped in `useMemo`, it only re-runs when the messages array changes. Even with hundreds of messages, this is negligible.

**Accessibility**: The expand/collapse button should get an `aria-expanded` attribute and an `aria-label` for screen readers. Add to the button in `CondensedToolGroup`:

```tsx
aria-expanded={expanded}
aria-label={`${group.summary}, click to ${expanded ? "collapse" : "expand"}`}
```

### 11. Alternative Considered and Rejected

**Regex-based detection at render time**: Parse `content` with a regex like `/^\[tool_(use|result):.*\]$/` per line. This is fragile -- if the text format of `extractText` changes, the regex breaks. Adding structured metadata (`toolCalls`, `isToolOnly`) to `ChatMessage` is more robust and self-documenting.

**Modifying message data in useWebSocket**: The requirement says the condenser should work at the rendering layer. Adding metadata to `ChatMessage` at creation time is a minor hook change, but the actual grouping/condensing happens entirely in the rendering layer. The underlying `messages` array is unchanged -- still the same `ChatMessage[]` with the same content strings. The raw view is completely unaffected.

---

